{"type":"text","timestamp":"2026-09-30T20:05:28.150Z","data":{"text":"Done. Summary of what I implemented for task 1:\n\n**`tests/git-test-env.ts`** (new) — exports `withoutBackgroundGit(env)` which appends `maintenance.auto=false` via `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT`/`GIT_CONFIG_KEY_<n>`/`GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_<n>` after any existing entries, returns the env unchanged if `maintenance.auto` is already configured, and applies itself to `process.env` on import.\n\n**`tests/git-background-work.test.ts`** (new) — enforces the delta's five scenarios:\n- appends after existing config\n- second application is a no-op\n- traced commit under the test env starts no `maintenance` child\n- traced control commit (config stripped, `-c maintenance.auto=true`) records `git maintenance run --auto`\n- both `--test` parts of the `package.json` `test` script carry the preload\n\n**`package.json`** — both `node --test` invocations now run `--import ./tests/git-test-env.ts` right after `--import tsx`.\n\nVerification: task verify passes (5/5), and `pnpm verify` (typechecks, build, full suite, lint) passes.\n\nResult written to `openspec/changes/124-ci-temp-repo-cleanup/.run/results/1.md`. One deviation noted there: the TS import uses `./git-test-env.js` because NodeNext/`tsc` rejects a literal `.ts` specifier (TS5097) without `allowImportingTsExtensions`; tsx resolves it to the same module."}}
